1
00:00:00,643 --> 00:00:03,860
An invoice can pass a calculation check and still contain the wrong

2
00:00:03,895 --> 00:00:07,899
numbers. I built Document Verification Lab to see what an independent

3
00:00:07,934 --> 00:00:10,631
visual check adds to document automation.

4
00:00:10,666 --> 00:00:13,952
Start with this generated invoice and its payment details.

5
00:00:13,988 --> 00:00:17,668
For a controlled stress test, I multiply every amount by ten.

6
00:00:17,703 --> 00:00:21,059
Subtotal, shipping and tax still add up to the total, so the

7
00:00:21,094 --> 00:00:24,496
arithmetic check passes. Comparing the extracted values with the

8
00:00:24,532 --> 00:00:26,765
original page reveals the problem.

9
00:00:26,800 --> 00:00:32,193
OpenBMB's MiniCPM OCR Jev takes the source image, candidate text, and a

10
00:00:32,228 --> 00:00:33,952
rendered copy of that text.

11
00:00:33,988 --> 00:00:37,726
Its two-billion-parameter classifier returns a consistency score.

12
00:00:37,761 --> 00:00:40,862
Higher means stronger predicted agreement with the pixels.

13
00:00:40,897 --> 00:00:44,126
Here, the score drops from about zero point nine eight to zero

14
00:00:44,161 --> 00:00:48,165
point zero zero seven. I also ran real OCR on one hundred

15
00:00:48,200 --> 00:00:52,251
and twenty generated invoices. Forty set the review threshold; eighty

16
00:00:52,286 --> 00:00:55,411
were held out. Rules caught all three test errors.

17
00:00:55,446 --> 00:00:59,184
Adding the verifier sent thirteen correct results to review, without

18
00:00:59,219 --> 00:01:02,332
catching another error. The separate stress tests tell a different

19
00:01:02,367 --> 00:01:06,395
story. All twenty-four internally consistent currency and amount changes

20
00:01:06,430 --> 00:01:10,179
passed the rules. The visual verifier flagged every one.

21
00:01:10,214 --> 00:01:13,686
That gives us a specific capability to investigate on representative

22
00:01:13,721 --> 00:01:17,610
documents. For supplier-invoice workflows, I would keep the source

23
00:01:17,645 --> 00:01:21,406
evidence attached, measure the extra errors caught per review, and check

24
00:01:21,441 --> 00:01:24,994
purchase orders separately. You can inspect every case and change the

25
00:01:25,029 --> 00:01:26,765
threshold in the open lab.

26
00:01:26,800 --> 00:01:29,311
The code, inputs and results are public.
